# Database migrations

Migrations live in `notifier/db/migrations`, run with goose as `notifier_migrator`, and are applied by
`make migrate`, which also regenerates `notifier/db/schema/`.

## Files and execution scope

- One file per short DDL change: `{nnn}__{snake_case_description}.sql`, the next free number.
- Forward-only: no `-- +goose Down`. A rollback is a new migration.
- Preserve merged or applied migration history. Before proposing an exception, inventory where
  the version was applied and obtain explicit approval for that exact history change. Unknown
  environments are not evidence that a rewrite is safe. Document both already-applied and fresh
  installation paths; a later migration cannot undo the locks taken by an earlier one.
- Automatic rollout migrations contain short DDL and bounded read-only schema assertions only.
  Catalog existence/shape checks are allowed; application-table scans are not. Existing-table
  `ALTER TABLE` must not
  add, set, change or drop a `DEFAULT`. No data writes (`INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, `MERGE`),
  backfill, `VALIDATE CONSTRAINT`, application-table scan or table rewrite belongs in this execution path, even in a separate transaction/file.
  Put that work in a reviewed asynchronous production runbook outside goose and service startup.
- A new `CREATE TABLE` may declare the final columns, defaults, nullability and checks directly:
  it has no historical rows to convert. This does not permit default changes through `ALTER`.
- No grants: tables are granted to the mode roles by default privileges from `notifier/db/init`.
- Schema-qualify every object: `notifier.t_notification`, not `t_notification`.
- Comments follow `ai/rules/comments.mdc`.

## Naming

All names are singular and in snake_case. `<table>` below is the table name without the `t_` prefix.

| Object | Pattern | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Table | `t_<name>` | `t_notification` |
| Function | `f_<name>` | `f_notification_stop` |
| Sequence | `s_<table>_<column>` | `s_notification_id` |
| Index | `ix_<table>_<column>` | `ix_notification_next_scheduled_dt` |
| Primary key | `pk_<table>_<column>` | `pk_notification_id` |
| Foreign key | `fk_<table>_<column>` | `fk_notification_client_id` |
| Unique | `uq_<table>_<column>` | `uq_notification_client_id_external_id` |
| Check | `ck_<table>_<column>` | `ck_notification_external_id` |

- A multi-column constraint or index joins the columns: `uq_notification_client_id_external_id`.
- A name longer than 63 characters is cut by PostgreSQL: shorten the column or table part.

## Types

- No `varchar(n)`: use `text` with a named length check. On an existing table, add the check
  `NOT VALID`; its validation belongs to the asynchronous production runbook.
- No `interval`: an integer with the unit in the name (`max_lifetime_sec`, `callback_timeout_ms`).
- Timestamps are `timestamptz` with the `_dt` suffix.

## Short DDL on existing tables

Most structural `ALTER TABLE` operations, including adding a column or check, require
`ACCESS EXCLUSIVE`. The lock blocks reads and writes and remains held until the transaction ends.
`lock_timeout` bounds waiting to acquire a lock, not how long an acquired lock is held.
`statement_timeout` bounds one statement, not the whole transaction. Use a short transaction
with a transaction deadline as well as bounded lock waits. For PostgreSQL 18, for example:

```sql
-- +goose Up
SET LOCAL lock_timeout = '1s';
SET LOCAL statement_timeout = '5s';
SET LOCAL transaction_timeout = '5s';
LOCK TABLE notifier.t_notification IN ACCESS EXCLUSIVE MODE;
ALTER TABLE notifier.t_notification ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS priority smallint;
ALTER TABLE notifier.t_notification DROP CONSTRAINT IF EXISTS ck_notification_priority;
ALTER TABLE notifier.t_notification
    ADD CONSTRAINT ck_notification_priority CHECK (priority BETWEEN 0 AND 9) NOT VALID;
```

- Goose SQL migrations are transactional by default: the file ends with a commit. The example
  adds a nullable column without a default, and does no data conversion or validation scan.
  Choose rollout-specific budgets; these values are examples.
- `SET LOCAL` applies only inside its transaction. Reapply it in every new transaction.
- `-- +goose StatementBegin` / `StatementEnd` group parser statements; they do not commit.
  Savepoints and loop iterations are not phase boundaries. `NO TRANSACTION`, explicit commits,
  `DO`, functions and dynamic SQL do not exempt an automatic migration from the DDL-only rule.
- A timeout aborts the DDL transaction. Release it and retry later; mark the migration version
  applied only after all its DDL has committed. Applying this version does not prove that the
  separately scheduled production work is complete.
- Tables created in this transaction need no explicit lock. A table created in an earlier
  committed migration is an existing table, including on a fresh installation.

`NOT VALID` immediately enforces a check for new or updated rows. It only skips checking old rows;
it does not make arbitrary legacy writes compatible. This example's check permits `NULL`.

## Asynchronous production work

Backfill, validation, default changes and other scans/rewrites must run independently of automatic
migrations and service startup. The runbook names the operator/job, exact SQL, execution budgets,
progress/completion queries, interruption/retry procedure and application-version gates. Do not
start this work from goose, wait for it in `migrate`, or hide it in an invoked function/script.

Keep real transaction boundaries even in the separate executor:

1. Commit the short expansion DDL before starting data work.
2. Execute each backfill batch in its own bounded transaction. Bound both changed rows and elapsed
   time; preserve a restart predicate and monitor progress. Do not wrap the loop in one transaction.
3. Validate only after the required backfill completion check succeeds. Use a separate transaction
   with `SHARE UPDATE EXCLUSIVE`, never an explicit `ACCESS EXCLUSIVE` lock.
4. Run required `SET/DROP DEFAULT` and other final metadata changes as separately reviewed short
   production transactions with lock and execution budgets. They stay outside automatic `ALTER`.

Example **manual/asynchronous backfill transaction**, not a goose migration. The separate executor
repeats it and commits each batch before starting the next:

```sql
BEGIN;
SET LOCAL lock_timeout = '1s';
SET LOCAL statement_timeout = '5s';
SET LOCAL transaction_timeout = '5s';
WITH batch AS (
    SELECT id FROM notifier.t_notification
    WHERE priority IS NULL
    ORDER BY id
    LIMIT 1000
    FOR UPDATE
)
UPDATE notifier.t_notification AS notification
SET priority = 0
FROM batch
WHERE notification.id = batch.id AND notification.priority IS NULL;
COMMIT;
```

The predicate makes committed batches safe to repeat. The row limit bounds writes; the deadline
also bounds a slow scan. A zero-row batch plus the completion predicate establishes completion
only when writers cannot introduce new unfilled values. With `SKIP LOCKED`, zero updated rows
alone is insufficient because locked matching rows may remain.

Example **manual/asynchronous validation transaction**, not a goose migration:

```sql
BEGIN;
SET LOCAL lock_timeout = '1s';
SET LOCAL statement_timeout = '30min';
SET LOCAL transaction_timeout = '30min';
ALTER TABLE notifier.t_notification VALIDATE CONSTRAINT ck_notification_priority;
COMMIT;
```

`SHARE UPDATE EXCLUSIVE` permits ordinary reads and writes. A failed validation leaves the earlier
`NOT VALID` constraint in place; correct the offending rows and retry without undoing completed
batches. The example budgets must be chosen for the actual table and production workload.

## Indexes on existing tables

Build a new index on an existing table asynchronously in production outside goose:

```sql
CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY IF NOT EXISTS ix_notification_external_id
    ON notifier.t_notification (external_id);
```

A failed build may leave an `INVALID` index that `IF NOT EXISTS` skips. Inspect its validity and
exact definition; if invalid, run `DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY IF EXISTS ix_notification_external_id`
and rebuild. Record completion before deploying code that depends on the index. Do not put a
plain `CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS` fallback in an automatic migration: a missing index would
cause the expensive build during rollout.

**Tell the owner about the asynchronous build** and include its exact command, verification and
retry steps under "Manual steps before rollout" in the PR description. Indexes created together
with a new table in the same migration may be created directly.

## Compatibility and verification

For every multi-phase change, record:

1. The application versions allowed to read/write each intermediate schema. Preserve legacy
   fields until their users have stopped; handle nullable and partially filled values. New writes
   must obey `NOT VALID` checks. If compatibility is not implemented, stop incompatible services
   through the transition and explicitly document downtime; do not claim a rolling upgrade.
2. The exact automatic DDL and separate production job order. A completed goose version is not
   the async job's completion signal. Gate dependent application behavior on verified job results.
3. Restart behavior after every commit and failed batch/validation. Do not reset converted data
   or remove source fields before conversion is proved complete.
4. PostgreSQL evidence from separate sessions: inspect `pg_locks`, prove the DDL commit releases
   `ACCESS EXCLUSIVE`, and show concurrent reads/writes during the independent backfill and
   validation. Check lock-wait failure and retry; elapsed time alone is not proof.
5. Both upgrades with representative legacy rows and fresh installations. Already-applied
   versions stay applied; changing an initial `CREATE TABLE` does not upgrade an existing table.

## Checker and lock-test contract

The task 14 checker/fixtures own automated rule checks. Keep one checker and one fixture suite.

- Reject automatic migrations containing existing-table `ALTER ... DEFAULT`, `UPDATE`/backfill,
  `VALIDATE`, scans/rewrites or existing-table index builds. Separate transactions/files and
  `NO TRANSACTION` do not make them acceptable. Inspect executed `DO`, function calls, scripts
  and dynamic SQL rather than accepting work hidden behind another statement. Classify actual
  operations, not keywords inside comments/strings or an uninvoked function definition.
- Accept final defaults/checks in a new `CREATE TABLE` and short existing-table DDL without a
  default, such as adding a nullable column and a check `NOT VALID` with bounded locks.
- Treat the manual/asynchronous SQL examples above as a separate execution path, never goose
  migration fixtures. Verify their actual commits and locks from another PostgreSQL session:
  DDL releases `ACCESS EXCLUSIVE` before independent data work, backfill/validation permit normal
  concurrent reads/writes, and lock-wait failure is safely retryable.

Static checks cannot prove execution boundaries, compatibility or restart safety. Keep the actual
PostgreSQL and migration-specific upgrade checks as well.

## Rerunnable changes

A migration must succeed even if its allowed DDL was already applied by hand.

- Create with `IF NOT EXISTS`: `CREATE TABLE`, `CREATE INDEX` on a new table, `ADD COLUMN`.
- Drop with `IF EXISTS`: `DROP TABLE`, `DROP INDEX`, `DROP COLUMN`, `DROP CONSTRAINT`.
- `ADD CONSTRAINT` has no `IF NOT EXISTS`: drop the constraint `IF EXISTS` first, as above.
- Functions use `CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION`; inspect the body and callers for forbidden work.
- Data inserts/conversion belong to a separate production procedure with explicit idempotency;
  `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` does not turn data work into short DDL.

## Checklist

1. Names follow the table above; everything is singular and schema-qualified.
2. Every automatic statement is rerunnable short DDL or a bounded schema assertion; no
   existing-table `ALTER ... DEFAULT`,
   backfill, `UPDATE`, `VALIDATE`, scan/rewrite or existing-table index build.
3. Existing-table DDL sets local lock/statement/transaction budgets and takes its explicit lock.
4. Required asynchronous work has a separate runbook, bounded batches, real commits, completion
   checks and retry behavior. Application compatibility and rollout gates are documented.
5. Manual production steps, including index builds, are shown to the owner and listed in the PR.
6. No `varchar`, no `interval`, no grants, no `Down`.
7. `make migrate` passes twice in a row and `notifier/db/schema/` is regenerated.
8. Real PostgreSQL checks verify phase locks, concurrent access, timeout/retry and upgrade paths.
